 ## Overview
-Deformation and Dynamic Modeling extends static radiance field representations to handle temporal changes, enabling the reconstruction and rendering of dynamic 3D scenes from video inputs. It solves the problem of under-constrained geometry in dynamic scenes by introducing time as an explicit input and using deformation fields, scene flow, or canonical space mappings to align temporal observations. Unlike static NeRF variants, these methods explicitly model motion, distinguishing themselves from siblings like [Relighting and Shading](17925-relighting-and-shading.md) which handle lighting changes, or [View Synthesis and Morphing](17926-view-synthesis-and-morphing.md) which may focus on interpolation without explicit physical motion modeling.
